<%# The bar rests visible, then retires — nav_autohide holds the timing and the
    reasoning. The hidden class is passed rather than hardcoded so the stylesheet
    keeps naming its own states.

    zone is what brings it back on a mouse, and without it the bar left and
    stayed gone: the hidden state is translateY(-100%) with opacity 0, so the
    element is off-screen and pointerenter — the only other mouse route to
    reveal — can never fire on it. Touch had document-level touchstart and the
    keyboard had focusin; the mouse had nothing. 64px is the band the cursor
    arrives in when it goes looking for navigation, the same way a video
    player's chrome behaves.

    hide-on-scroll because scrolling is the reader saying they are reading the
    page rather than the bar, and waiting out a timer after that is the bar
    overstaying a decision already made. %>
<div class="nav_swiper" data-controller="nav-autohide"
     data-nav-autohide-zone-value="64"
     data-nav-autohide-hide-on-scroll-value="true"
     data-nav-autohide-hidden-class="nav_swiper--hidden">
  <%# These are links that navigate — another host entirely — and there is no
      tabpanel anywhere on the page. Dressing them as role="tablist" >
      role="group" > role="tab" produced three axe violations at once: the group
      broke the tablist's required children, the tabs lost their required
      tablist parent through it, and role="tablist" on <nav> overrode the
      navigation landmark, so everything inside stopped counting as landmark
      content. It is a navigation bar, so it says so; aria-current marks the
      active entry, which is the correct signal for a nav.

      One flat run, no groups — operator, 2026-08-29. The two Hick's-law groups
      were carrying nothing visible since the divider between them came out, and
      the bar is eight entries now rather than eleven, which is the size the
      grouping existed to buy. %>
  <%# Labelled as the primary navigation because that is what it is: the layout
      calls this bar the canonical primary nav, the left rail that restated it
      was removed on that reasoning, and it is the only landmark carrying the
      verticals at desktop width. It announced itself as "Seksjoner" instead,
      which named a part of the page rather than the navigation of the site, and
      left the page with no primary navigation landmark at all. %>
  <nav id="nav_sections" class="nav_swiper_bar" aria-label="<%= t("nav.primary_nav") %>" data-brgen-shell-target="navSections">
    <div class="nav_swiper_group nav_swiper_group--verticals">
      <% brgen_nav_items.each do |slug, label, href| %>
        <% active = nav_item_active?(slug) %>
        <%# Every entry opens in a new tab — operator instruction 2026-08-10.
            rel="noopener" is not optional with target="_blank": without it the
            opened page gets a window.opener handle back into this one. The
            verticals are separate hosts, so this is cross-origin.
            aria-label carries the "new tab" fact, because a sighted user gets
            it from the tab appearing and a screen-reader user gets nothing. %>
        <%= link_to href,
              class: "nav_link#{" active" if active}",
              target: "_blank",
              rel: "noopener",
              "aria-label": t("nav.opens_new_tab", label: label, default: "%{label} (opens in a new tab)"),
              "aria-current": ("page" if active) do %>
          <%= label %>
          <%# The badge is inside the link so it cannot be separated from what it
              qualifies, and it is a <sup> because that is what it is: a mark on
              a word. Its text is real content, not a ::after — a reader who
              cannot see the colour still gets told the surface is new, and the
              aria-label above already names the destination, so this reads as
              the qualifier it is rather than as part of the place name. %>
          <% if (badge = nav_item_badge(slug)) %>
            <sup class="nav_link_badge"><%= badge %></sup>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
    <%# The feed orderings are not here any more. One rule each, and it is the
        rule rather than the row that was the problem: this bar is how you leave
        this app — eight verticals, each a separate host — and the bottom tab bar
        is how you move within it. Hot, New, Following and Communities are
        neither. They order the feed, so they sit with the feed, in
        home/index.html.erb.

        This is not a return to the two-scroller shape test_root_offers_the_
        verticals_once forbids. That row listed the same verticals as this bar,
        immediately under it; a feed ordering duplicates nothing here, and the
        control it moved to is `chips` — four links that wrap, not a scroller. %>
  </nav>
</div>
